<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Design doc section 11, step 5: "Legacy runner. net472, same attribute contract, no shared
    assembly." See docs/03-architecture/SAD.md §9 for what this closes and what it still doesn't (real AppDomain
    isolation between co-hosted legacy plugins — see Hosting/LegacyPluginLoadContext.cs).

    The two PackageReferences below (System.Text.Json, System.Threading.Channels) are a deliberate,
    narrow exception to the runner's own zero-PackageReference rule - the rule that exists so a PLUGIN
    has nothing of the runner's to version-collide against (contracts/EnlistAttributes.cs's own header
    comment). On net10.0 both are already part of the BCL, which is why the modern Enlist.Runner needs
    neither reference at all; net472 simply does not ship them, and this project's runner<->agent wire
    protocol (Protocol/) needs both.

    This comment used to say the two were "not visible to, or loaded into the same resolution scope
    as, any plugin assembly". That is not true on net472 and was corrected on 2026-09-13. There is no
    AssemblyLoadContext here: Hosting/LegacyPluginLoadContext loads plugins with Assembly.LoadFrom
    into this process's ONE AppDomain, where the runner's own System.Text.Json is already loaded. A
    net472 plugin that carries its own copy at a different version is in a genuine collision with it,
    and no binding redirects are generated for this executable, so the CLR resolves it by whichever
    identity satisfies the reference rather than by who asked.

    What limits the damage is the process boundary, not the load context: every application gets its
    own runner process, so the surface is one plugin against these two assemblies - never plugin
    against plugin. It is still a real gap for a net472 plugin that ships System.Text.Json, which is
    not an exotic thing for one to do. See Hosting/LegacyPluginLoadContext.cs and
    docs/03-architecture/SAD.md section 9.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!--
      Deliberately the SAME canonical name the modern Enlist.Runner uses ("enlist-runner"), not a
      "-legacy"-suffixed one: RunnerStaging (Enlist.Agent) looks for exactly "enlist-runner.exe" in
      whichever runner-bin directory it's given (the one named by enlist-agent's runner-bin or
      legacy-runner-bin argument); which flavor an agent gets is already determined entirely by WHICH
      directory it's pointed at (a separate, non-colliding path per flavor), so the exe within it
      doesn't need its own flavor-specific name too.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>enlist-runner</AssemblyName>
    <TargetFramework>net472</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Enlist.Runner.Legacy</RootNamespace>
    <!-- The iC mark on the legacy enlist-runner.exe, as on the modern one; see src\branding. -->
    <ApplicationIcon>..\branding\enlist.ico</ApplicationIcon>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Text.Json" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Threading.Channels" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
